insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2725, $q$Let People Be More Than a Label$q$, $q$let-people-be-more-than-a-label$q$, $pt$I want to leave room for people to change. If I reduce someone to a job, a mistake, or a reputation, I may stop listening before they speak. A label can become the only thing I see, even when a person is taking a new step.

Matthew was a tax collector who became one of Jesus's twelve closest followers. When Jesus called him, he was sitting at his collection booth. This was near Capernaum (kuh-PERR-nay-uhm), a lakeside town in what is now northern Israel. Its ruins stand on the northwestern shore of the Sea of Galilee.

Tax collectors worked within the system of rulers who controlled the region. Their work brought them suspicion and resentment. The story does not list Matthew's personal offenses. It does show how strongly some people objected to sharing a table with people in his line of work.

Jesus spoke two words to Matthew: “Follow me” (Matthew 9:9, NIV). Matthew stood up and went with him.

The next scene is a meal. Jesus and his followers ate with tax collectors and others described as sinners. Some religious teachers questioned why Jesus would eat with such people (Matthew 9:10–11).

Jesus answered by comparing his work to a doctor's care. People who are sick need help. He also pointed the teachers toward mercy (Matthew 9:12–13). He made room at the table for people who needed a different direction in life.

What catches my attention is that Jesus addressed Matthew as someone who could respond. Matthew's occupation was part of his story, but it did not prevent an invitation. The response belonged to Matthew, and he took it.

That challenges the way I might describe someone else. Calling a coworker unreliable can end the conversation before I ask what happened. I can name the missed deadline clearly, explain its effect, and ask what would help us keep the next agreement. The problem stays visible. So does the person.

Leaving room for change does not require instant trust. Trust can grow as someone follows through. I can pay attention to that evidence instead of repeating an old judgment every time their name comes up.

Matthew's story invites me to look again. Is there someone I have stopped seeing because I think I already know the whole story?

I want my next conversation to leave room for an answer I have not heard yet.$pt$, $q$2026-11-22T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 44, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
