insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2718, $q$Give Care a Practical Form$q$, $q$give-care-a-practical-form$q$, $pt$When someone depends on me, love needs a practical form. Concern matters, but it cannot make a meal or arrange a ride by itself. I want to notice what care requires and do the part that is within my reach.

Jochebed (JAHK-uh-bed) was the mother of Moses, who would later lead the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt. His life began under a threat. Egypt's king had ordered that newborn Hebrew boys be thrown into the Nile River (Exodus 1:22).

Her story belongs to the world of the Nile Delta in northern Egypt, where the river spreads toward the sea north of modern Cairo. The Bible does not identify the exact riverbank where she acted.

Jochebed hid her baby for three months. When she could no longer keep him hidden, she made a basket from river plants and sealed it against the water. She placed him among the reeds at the river's edge. His older sister watched from a distance (Exodus 2:1–4).

The king's daughter found the crying child and felt compassion for him. His sister offered to find a woman to nurse him. She brought back his own mother. Jochebed cared for her son until he was older, then brought him to the king's daughter, who adopted him (Exodus 2:5–10).

Hebrews remembers that Moses's parents acted “by faith” (Hebrews 11:23, NIV). Their faith had something to do with the way they protected a child.

I notice how practical the care was. There was hiding, a basket made ready, and a place chosen among the reeds. Exodus describes a child placed at the bank, with his sister watching. Each detail matters because this was a real life in danger.

The story makes me look at the gap between feeling concerned and offering useful help. A person caring for a sick relative may need groceries brought to the door. A neighbor with a broken car may need a ride. I can ask what would help most and listen carefully before deciding what to offer.

I cannot promise to solve everything. I can be clear about what I can offer, agree on a time, and follow through. That makes my help something another person can count on.

Jochebed's story leaves me with a simple question: what does care need to become in my hands today?

I want to answer with something I can actually do.$pt$, $q$2026-11-15T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 45, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
