insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2704, $q$Leave Room for an Answer$q$, $q$leave-room-for-an-answer$q$, $pt$When I ask someone for help, I want to understand something I may have missed. That takes more than asking a question. I need to leave room for an answer that could change what I do next.

Cornelius (kor-NEE-lee-us) was a Roman army officer. He lived in Caesarea (see-zuh-REE-uh), a port on the coast of present-day Israel, about thirty miles north of Tel Aviv. He prayed regularly and gave generously to people in need (Acts 10:1–2).

An angel told him to send for Peter, one of Jesus's followers. Cornelius sent messengers and gathered his relatives and close friends. He was ready to hear what Peter would say (Acts 10:3–8, 24).

This meeting crossed a real divide. Peter was Jewish. Cornelius was not. Before the visit, God had shown Peter that he should not treat another person as unclean. Peter came into the house, and Cornelius explained why he had sent for him (Acts 10:28–33).

The officer did not use the gathering to give orders. He told Peter that they were there before God to listen. Peter, too, had something to learn. He said, “God does not show favoritism” (Acts 10:34, NIV).

Peter spoke about Jesus's life, death, and resurrection. As he spoke, the Holy Spirit came upon those listening. Peter called for them to be baptized. They asked him to stay for a few days (Acts 10:36–48).

What stays with me is the room Cornelius made for that message. His work involved authority. In his home, he took the place of a learner. His prayers led to a practical response: he sent for someone, welcomed him, and listened.

I can ask for advice and still have my defense ready before the other person finishes. I can invite someone to explain a problem while hoping they will confirm the answer I already prefer. The question sounds open. My response may close it.

Cornelius gives me a different picture. Being responsible for others does not mean I have nothing left to receive. I can listen without losing my responsibility to think carefully about what I hear.

At the next conversation where I need help, I can ask a clear question and let the answer finish. Then I can name what I learned and decide what needs to change.

I want my willingness to learn to show up in what I do afterward.$pt$, $q$2026-11-08T22:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 46, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
