insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2687, $q$When Faith Becomes a Bargain$q$, $q$when-faith-becomes-a-bargain$q$, $pt$Faith matters to me because I need help seeing beyond what I want. But I can turn prayer into a bargain: I will do the right religious things, and God will make my plans work. That leaves little room for God to change the plans—or me.

Micah (MY-kuh) was a man who lived in Israel before it had kings. He is not the later prophet whose name appears on a Bible book. His home was in the hills of Ephraim (EE-fray-im), part of the central hill country north of Jerusalem, in today's West Bank. Its exact location is unknown.

His story starts with stolen silver. Micah admitted taking it from his mother and returned it. She then used some of it to have an idol made. It went into his house, where he had his own place of worship (Judges 17:1–5).

Later, Micah hired a young man from Israel's priestly tribe to serve as his priest. Micah took this as a reason to expect God's favor: “Now I know that the Lord will be good to me” (Judges 17:13, NIV).

The next chapter tests that confidence. Armed men from the tribe of Dan took his religious objects. They offered his priest a job serving their whole tribe, and the priest went with them. Micah protested, but the men threatened him. Seeing that they were stronger, he went home (Judges 18:14–26).

The story does not make the theft right. It shows how little protection Micah's arrangement gave him. The things he had gathered, and the priest he paid, could be carried away.

I see a warning here about treating faith as something I can arrange around my own wishes. A gift to a church does not buy God's agreement. A prayer before a decision does not mean I have listened for an answer I might dislike.

For me, listening begins with an honest question: am I willing to change course? I can read the passage that challenges me, hear the concern I would rather dismiss, and admit when a choice serves me at someone else's expense.

I still want to bring my hopes to God. I also want to leave room for correction. Faith can help me face an uncertain outcome without pretending I have purchased a certain one.

That is a different prayer from asking God to protect every plan I have already made.$pt$, $q$2026-10-25T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 48, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
