insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2666, $q$An Apology Needs a Next Step$q$, $q$an-apology-needs-a-next-step$q$, replace($pt$When I hurt someone, saying I am sorry is a beginning. The person I hurt also has to live with what I do next. If I keep making the same choice, my words ask them to carry the cost of a change I have not made.

Manasseh (man-ASS-eh) was a king of Judah, the small kingdom centered on Jerusalem. He ruled in the seventh century BC, more than 2,600 years ago. The city remains in the same hills today.

His story includes terrible harm. He brought the worship of other gods into God's temple. He sacrificed his children and caused the deaths of innocent people. These were acts with victims, not just errors in judgment (2 Chronicles 33:3–6[[semicolon]] 2 Kings 21:16).

The book of Chronicles says God warned Manasseh and his people, but they did not listen. An invading army captured him and took him to Babylon. That ancient city's ruins are in modern Iraq, about 53 miles south of Baghdad.

In captivity, Manasseh asked God for help. The account says he “humbled himself greatly” (2 Chronicles 33:12, NIV). God heard his prayer and brought him back to Jerusalem.

Then his actions began to change. He removed the idols and the altars he had built for other gods. He restored the Lord's altar and called the people to worship God (2 Chronicles 33:15–16).

That change matters. So do the lives he had already harmed. His return could not make those deaths unhappen. The book of Kings keeps his violence in the record. I do not want to rush past those people to reach a comfortable ending.

What I see in Manasseh is a reason to stop defending a wrong choice. I can admit what I did without first finding someone whose actions were worse. I can ask God for mercy and still accept responsibility for repair.

For me, that might mean correcting a false statement in front of the people who heard it. It might mean returning money, changing a decision, or asking what would help without demanding an immediate answer. The next step should address the actual harm.

The person I hurt may need time or distance. My apology does not give me the right to set their timetable for trust.

I cannot rewrite what happened. I can stop adding to it. That is where I want an apology to become visible: in a different choice, made again when no one is applauding.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), $q$2026-10-11T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 50, null, null, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
on conflict (post_id) do update set post_title = excluded.post_title, slug = excluded.slug, post_text = excluded.post_text, publish_date = excluded.publish_date, post_status = excluded.post_status, priority = excluded.priority, wordpress_url = coalesce(excluded.wordpress_url, songs.wordpress_url), substack_url = coalesce(excluded.substack_url, songs.substack_url), podcast_url = coalesce(excluded.podcast_url, songs.podcast_url), podcast_title = coalesce(excluded.podcast_title, songs.podcast_title), youtube_url = coalesce(excluded.youtube_url, songs.youtube_url);
insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2656, $q$Remember Who Helped You$q$, $q$remember-who-helped-you$q$, $pt$I want to notice the people whose daily work makes my life possible. A meal, a finished project, a safe place to sleep: someone helped make each of those happen. When I forget that help, I can start treating everything I have as something I produced alone.

Nabal (NAY-bul) was a wealthy livestock owner in ancient Judah. His sheep were being sheared at Carmel (KAR-mul), a town usually identified with a site about eight miles south-southeast of Hebron (HEE-brun), in today's West Bank.

David, who would later become Israel's king, was living in the wilderness with his followers. They had helped protect Nabal's shepherds. At shearing time, David sent men to ask Nabal for food. He invited Nabal to check with his own workers about the help they had received (1 Samuel 25:4–8).

Nabal refused and insulted David. In his reply, he spoke of “my bread and water” and the meat prepared for his workers (1 Samuel 25:11, NIV). His answer made no room for the people who had helped keep his flocks safe.

David responded by preparing an armed attack. Nabal's insult did not excuse that choice. People throughout the household were now in danger.

One servant went to Nabal's wife, Abigail (AB-ih-gayl). He described the protection David's men had given them. She gathered food, met David, and urged him to stop. David accepted her appeal and turned back (1 Samuel 25:14–35).

While she was preventing bloodshed, Nabal was holding a feast. He did not learn what had happened until morning. The account ends with his death about ten days later (1 Samuel 25:36–38).

I do not read this as a promise that every refusal will bring disaster. What stays with me is how much Nabal's workers knew that his answer ignored. He had people nearby who could have helped him see the whole picture.

Before I decide that someone wants too much, I can ask what they have already contributed. Before I take credit, I can name the person who did the work. A thank-you becomes more useful when it is specific, and when fair treatment follows it.

I can still have limits. I cannot meet every request. But I want my answer to begin with an honest picture of what I have received. Remembering who helped me is one way to keep gratitude from becoming just a word.$pt$, $q$2026-10-04T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$future$q$, 51, null, null, null, null, null, $q$Nabal$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
