insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (2627, $q$Smart Don't Mean Good$q$, $q$smart-dont-mean-good$q$, replace($pt$I want my advice to help people, not just help someone win. That means asking why a plan matters before working out how to make it succeed. A smart plan can still hurt someone.

Ahithophel (uh-HITH-uh-fel) helped David, the king of ancient Israel, make hard choices. People trusted his advice. But when David's son tried to take the throne, Ahithophel chose the son's side (2 Samuel 15:12).

The son's name was Absalom (AB-suh-luhm). He wanted to replace his father as king. David fled Jerusalem, and Absalom entered the city with his followers. This was the same Jerusalem we can find today, in the hills about 32 miles east of the Mediterranean coast.

Ahithophel had a plan. He would take twelve thousand men and chase David that night. David was tired and weak. Ahithophel would kill him and bring his followers back to Absalom. The leaders who heard the plan liked it (2 Samuel 17:1–4).

The man who once helped David now offered to hunt him down.

But another adviser spoke. Hushai (HOO-shy) was David's friend. David had sent him back to work against Ahithophel's advice. Hushai urged Absalom to wait, gather more soldiers, and lead the army himself. Absalom chose his plan instead (2 Samuel 15:34[[semicolon]] 17:5–14).

The Bible says God had decided to stop “the good advice of Ahithophel” (2 Samuel 17:14, NIV).

That phrase can be confusing. Here, I understand “good” to mean the plan could work. It does not mean killing David was right. Ahithophel knew how to win. He was using that skill to help a son kill his father.

I can see a smaller version of that problem in daily life. A sharp reply might win an argument and wound a friend. Leaving someone out might make a project easier and cost that person a fair chance. Getting the result I want does not settle whether I should want it.

So before I offer advice, I need to name the purpose. Who would this help? Who might it hurt? Would I still support it if I were the person paying the price?

Those questions give me a way to pause. I can change the goal, suggest a kinder path, or refuse to help with harm.

I want to bring more than a clever answer to the table. I want a reason for giving it that I can stand behind.$pt$, '[[semicolon]]', chr(59)), $q$2026-09-20T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, 53, $q$https://stevesammons.com/smart-dont-mean-good/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/a-smart-plan-can-still-be-wrong$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/the-dangerous-gap-between-smart-and$q$, $q$The Dangerous Gap Between Smart and Good$q$, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
