insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1952, $q$When Doubt Steals Your Voice$q$, $q$when-doubt-steals-your-voice$q$, $pt$He had prayed for a son every day of his adult life.

Zechariah was an old priest, married to Elizabeth, and they were well past the age where the prayer made any practical sense. His division served at the temple two weeks out of the year. The incense offering was assigned by lot, and with hundreds of priests in the rotation, most got one turn in a lifetime. Plenty got none at all.

That morning the lot fell to him (Luke 1:9).

The single most sacred moment of his career. And while he's standing there in the Holy Place, an angel appears at the right side of the altar. Gabriel himself. "Your prayer has been heard. Your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son."

Zechariah's response?

"How can I be sure of this?" (Luke 1:18)

Wait.

He prayed for this. For decades. And now he's asking for proof.

Gabriel doesn't argue. He takes the man's voice. Nine months of silence.

That struck me as harsh, until I sat with it a while.

Zechariah's doubt wasn't curiosity. It was the thing I do — ask for something, then act surprised when it shows up. I've prayed for a door to open and then stood in the doorway questioning whether I was ready to walk through it. I've asked for the opportunity and spent the first week checking to see whether it was real.

The silence wasn't punishment. I've come to think it was an invitation.

Nine months to stop talking and start trusting. Nine months to watch the impossible unfold in his own house — Elizabeth further along every week, and no way to explain it to a soul. Nine months to get ready for what he'd been asking for all along.

When the boy was born, the neighbors assumed he'd carry his father's name. Elizabeth said no. John. They turned to the old man and handed him a tablet.

He wrote: "His name is John" (Luke 1:63).

His voice came back that instant.

But he wasn't the same man. The first words out of his mouth weren't complaints, and they weren't questions. They were worship — a prophecy spoken over the baby about preparing the way (Luke 1:67-79).

The silence had done its work.

Here's what I'm learning. My biggest obstacle usually isn't getting the thing I asked for. It's believing it when it arrives.

Sometimes the forced pause is what gets you ready to receive it.$pt$, $q$2026-08-30T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, 56, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-doubt-steals-your-voice/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-doubt-steals-your-voice$q$, null, null, null, null, $q$waiting$q$)
