insert into public.songs (post_id, post_title, slug, post_text, publish_date, post_status, priority, wordpress_url, substack_url, podcast_url, podcast_title, youtube_url, character, status) values (1903, $q$When Nobody Is Left to Tell You No$q$, $q$when-nobody-is-left-to-tell-you-no$q$, $pt$Most of us do not need more people who agree with us. We need at least one person who can tell us the truth when agreement would be easier.

That becomes harder as our influence grows. A leader may have advisers, employees, friends, and family nearby and still have no one who feels free to say, “You are wrong.”

King David learned the value of that voice after committing adultery with Bathsheba and arranging the death of her husband, Uriah. The prophet Nathan came to him with a story about a rich man who stole a poor man’s only lamb. David was furious at the injustice. Then Nathan made the story personal: “You are the man!” (2 Samuel 12:7).

Nathan’s words carried risk. David had already used his power to hide what he had done. Yet Nathan spoke, David confessed, and the truth interrupted the cover-up.

David’s son Solomon later became famous for wisdom. He organized a large government, built the temple in Jerusalem, and drew visitors from other nations. First Kings even records the names and jobs of his chief officials (1 Kings 4:1–6).

But success did not protect Solomon from drift. Early in his reign, he married the daughter of Egypt’s pharaoh as part of a political alliance (1 Kings 3:1). Later he accumulated many foreign wives, worshiped other gods, and ignored repeated warnings (1 Kings 11:1–10). After his death, the kingdom split.

The Bible does not say that Solomon lacked honest advisers. Still, the contrast makes me stop. David’s worst failure was met by a person who stood in the room and named it. In Solomon’s long decline, Scripture records warnings, but no similar scene of a trusted person breaking through.

That raises uncomfortable questions for us. Who can challenge a decision before it becomes a pattern? When did we last change course because someone spoke honestly? What happened to the last person who disagreed with us?

We may say we welcome correction. The people around us learn the truth by watching our response. If disagreement brings punishment, distance, or ridicule, silence will soon look safer.

Wisdom is not only knowing what to do. Sometimes it is giving another person enough standing to tell us what we cannot see—and enough safety to keep telling us.

That kind of honesty is worth protecting.$pt$, $q$2026-08-16T21:00:00Z$q$, $q$publish$q$, 58, $q$https://stevesammons.com/when-nobody-is-left-to-tell-you-no/$q$, $q$https://sammons.substack.com/p/when-nobody-is-left-to-tell-you-no$q$, null, null, null, $q$Solomon$q$, $q$done_before$q$)
